\description{
\code{nomo_invariance_longitudinal()} asks whether the same measures mean the same
thing each time the same people answer them. It fits the measurement model
at every occasion in one model and imposes increasingly strict equality
constraints across occasions, as \code{\link[=nomo_invariance]{nomo_invariance()}} does across groups.
}
\details{
\strong{Why.} A change in scores is a change in the construct only if the
measurement relations hold across occasions. Equal loadings let changes in
the construct's variance and relations be compared, and equal intercepts let
changes in its mean be compared (Widaman, Ferrer, & Conger, 2010). An item
whose meaning drifts, through practice, an intervention, or development,
otherwise shows up as change in the construct.

\strong{The model.} \code{model} describes one occasion, in the items' own names.
\code{columns} says how an item and an occasion name a column of \code{data}: with the
default \code{"{item}_{occasion}"}, item \code{w1} at occasion \code{t2} is column \code{w1_t2}.
Each factor is measured at every occasion, and the occasion factors are
correlated. Each item's unique factor is correlated with the same item's
unique factor on the other occasions, because what an item measures besides
the construct is usually stable too, and leaving those covariances out
biases the estimates (Widaman et al., 2010). \code{auto} sets how many lags they
span.

\strong{The sequence.} The levels, the ordered-item identification, partial
releases, and score diagnostics are those of \code{\link[=nomo_invariance]{nomo_invariance()}}, applied
across occasions instead of groups. A partial release names the item as in
\code{model}, and frees that parameter across all occasions: \code{"w3 ~ 1"} releases
the intercept of \code{w3}. A release naming a column, such as \code{"w3_t2 ~ 1"}, is
an error, as is any release that frees no parameter. For ordered items, Wu
and Estabrook's (2016) identification is the default; \code{ID.cat = "millsap"},
with \code{ID.fac = "UL"}, applies Millsap and Tein's (2004) conditions, which
Liu et al. (2017) extend to repeated measures. An item whose columns are
stored as ordered factors
is modeled as ordered on every occasion, whether or not \code{ordered} names it.

\strong{Latent change.} Once intercepts are invariant, fully or partially, the
construct's mean can be compared across occasions. Under the default
\code{ID.fac = "std.lv"} the first occasion's latent mean is 0 and its variance
1, so \code{latent_means} gives each later occasion's latent mean as a change in
the first occasion's latent standard deviations, with its interval.
}
